#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Test the new desktop and AI features on a live Umoja session, on camera.

Run it from a terminal inside an Umoja session (the test laptop, not a
machine you're working on), then keep your hands off the keyboard and mouse
until it says it's done. Each feature is driven the way a person would use
it: through Ask Umoja's rows, the assistant, notifications and real app
windows. Then the script checks what actually happened: the setting changed
and changed back, the emoji landed in the app's text, the screenshot got a
name, and so on.

    tools/test/umoja-feature-test                 # everything
    tools/test/umoja-feature-test --only emoji rewrite
    tools/test/umoja-feature-test --list

It writes, by default to ~/umoja-feature-test-<time>/:

    REPORT.md         pass / fail / skip per test, every check, with pictures
    results.json      the same, for a machine (or Claude) to read
    images/*.png      screenshots at the moments that matter (grim)
    videos/*.webm     a clip of each test (wf-recorder), with the sound the
                      computer played (the default output's monitor)
    audio/*.wav       what read-aloud tests heard, as played
    journal.txt       the session's journal for the run (umoja units)
    log.txt           what the script did, step by step

Everything goes over D-Bus and Umoja's own tools (umoja-mcp). No keyboard or
mouse input is injected: text reaches apps only through the features under
test. The test hooks it needs (org.umoja.Overlay AskRows, AskActivate,
TestAction, AnswerConfirm) only answer while $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/umoja-demo
exists, which the script creates and removes. It holds an idle inhibitor,
checks before every test that the screen is neither blanked nor locked, and
puts back what it changes (dark style, Night Light, Do Not Disturb, activity
recall, the test setup and automation). Tests that use the assistant use
whatever model you have set up.
"""

